# ouroboros-ui — the variables this module reads.
#
# Copy it and edit what you need; the copy is git-ignored, this file is not:
#
#   cp ouroboros-ui/.env.example ouroboros-ui/.env
#
# **This module needs its own copy, and the repo-root .env will not do.** Every other
# service reads the repo-root .env and then its own (docs/CONVENTIONS.md § 4); this one
# reads neither by itself, because Next.js does the loading and Next.js loads .env files
# from the project directory only — this one. Without the copy, `yarn dev` from here
# starts a UI that throws `OURO_REST_URL is not set` on the first screen that calls the
# API, and the value has to be exported around it.
#
# Next.js looks a name up in this order, stopping at the first hit (its environment
# variables guide, bundled at node_modules/next/dist/docs/01-app/02-guides):
#
#   process.env  →  .env.<NODE_ENV>.local  →  .env.local  →  .env.<NODE_ENV>  →  .env
#
# The real process environment coming first is the same rule the other modules follow, and
# it buys the same two things: a container is configured by exactly what it was started
# with, and a single run can be overridden without editing anything:
#
#   OURO_REST_URL=http://localhost:4100 yarn dev
#
# The repo-root .env.example stays the complete list of every OURO_* variable the system
# reads; this is a subset of it, and the value below is identical to the one there.
#
# Everything here is a local development placeholder and is safe to commit. Nothing here
# is a real credential, and nothing here is suitable for a deployed environment.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Service wiring
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Base URL of ouroboros-rest — the only service this module may call. The UI reaches
# nothing else, not the database and not the engine, and that boundary is what keeps
# tenancy enforcement in one place (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md § 2).
#
# An absolute http or https URL; a trailing slash is trimmed for you. app/env.ts validates
# it and throws naming this variable when it is missing or malformed, rather than letting
# it surface as a confusing fetch error. It is read lazily, so `next build` still succeeds
# on a machine that has no reason to know this address.
#
# No NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, deliberately: Next.js therefore never inlines it into the browser
# bundle, and an address the browser can read is an address a tenant can point elsewhere.
# The API client runs on the server for that reason and forwards the HttpOnly session
# cookie itself (app/api/server.ts).
OURO_REST_URL=http://localhost:4000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two variables that belong to Next.js rather than here
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# PORT — the development default is 3000 (docs/CONVENTIONS.md § 4, port map), and it is
# deliberately not declared below because setting it here would do nothing. The `next` CLI
# resolves the listen port from the real process environment as it parses its arguments,
# which happens before Next.js loads this file, so a PORT line here is read too late and
# ignored in silence. Pass it instead:
#
#   PORT=3001 yarn dev          # or: yarn dev --port 3001
#
# NODE_ENV — set by Next.js itself: development for `next dev`, production for every other
# command. It is also what selects the .env.<NODE_ENV> files in the order above, so it has
# to be decided before they are read and cannot be one of them. This module reads it once,
# to mark the active-workspace cookie Secure outside development (app/api/server.ts).
